The trick to understanding raw pointer declarations is to read them backwards:
const int * const * * const * const value = 0;
What is that? It's a const pointer to a const pointer to an unconst pointer to a const pointer to a const int.

With the custom deleter for unique_ptr, you don't have to make a custom callable. You can pass the function pointer type as the second template parameter and a function pointer as a second constructor parameter:

std::unique_ptr<int, void(*)(void *)> ptr{
    static_cast<int *>(malloc(sizeof(int))) ,
    free
};

Important addition to the reinterpret_cast bit:
Yes, you can technically do
foo* f = get_some_foo_ptr();
void* vp = f;
bar* b = static_cast<bar*>(vp);
This will interpret the exact address of the object stored in f as an object of type bar. Do not do this unless you are 100% sure of what you are doing, because odds are trying to read the object of type foo as though it had type bar will lead to problems.

using namespace std is the first thing my c++ teacher in college told us NOT to do. He was a good teacher and to this day I always make sure I understand what a line of code is doing before using it because "using namespace std" is a prime example why using code because "thats what people do" is a bad idea. It still baffles me that so many beginner programming books use this...

