So this is a criticism based on NOT KNOWING MODERN C++ (or really any C++ beyond C++98)..

C++: 
std::vector v{1, 2};
std::println("{}", v);

The version on the left won't compile as a vector has to contain elements of the same type. You can do it with std::variant, which is type-safe. 

std::vector<std::variant<std::string, int>> varv{1, "2"};

You can print heterogeneous containers with std::visit. And all of this is compiled code, not runtime interpreted, which is one of the reasons why C++ is so much faster than Python..

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::vector<std::string> I = {"1", "2"};
    std::cout << "[";
    for (size_t i = 0; i < I.size(); ++i) {
        std::cout << I[i];
        if (i != I.size() - 1) std::cout << ", ";
    }
    std::cout << "]" << std::endl;
    return 0;
}

